  "body": "## What was missing\n\nFallback chains shipped in 4.31.0 with **no way to tell they had fired**. A request served by the second model in a chain looked identical to one served by a healthy primary — the row named the model that answered, which is correct, but nothing said it was not the one the route picked.\n\nSo a primary could be failing every single request, silently, and the dashboard would look fine.\n\n## The data\n\nTwo new columns, both migrated with `ALTER TABLE` for existing databases:\n\n- `route_attempt` — 0 for the route's own model, 1+ for a fallback\n- `route_primary_model` — recorded only when a later attempt answered, because otherwise it just repeats `resolved_model`\n\nThe executor already called back on every attempt so analytics could name the model that answered; it now passes the attempt index with it, and the capture remembers the first model it saw.\n\n## Surfaced three ways\n\n\"Did failover happen\" and \"what is failing\" are different questions:\n\n- a **Served by fallback** stat card, as count and share\n- a **Failover** panel listing `primary → what covered for it` by volume, so a consistently failing primary is obvious\n- a **badge on the affected row**, plus the full route in the detail panel\n\n## Old rows say nothing, not zero\n\nRows written before this version have no route data at all, which is **not** the same as \"no fallback was used\". The store counts non-null `route_attempt` separately and the card renders an em dash rather than a reassuring `0.0%` for traffic it knows nothing about — the same distinction the cache columns already make, and for the same reason.\n\n## Verification\n\nThrough the whole path with real data rather than per layer — drive `RequestCapture` the way the executor drives it (attempt 0, then attempt 1), finish the capture, read the row back out of SQLite, then read the aggregate:\n\n```\nprovider            cerebras\nresolved_model      qwen-3\nroute_attempt       1\nroute_primary_model nvidia_nim/nemotron\nserved_by_fallback  1 of 1 reported\nfallback_routes     [{'primary': 'nvidia_nim/nemotron', 'served_by': 'cerebras/qwen-3', 'count': 1}]\n```\n\n```\n$ uv run ty check\nAll checks passed!\n\n$ env -u ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS -u EXA_API_KEY uv run pytest tests/core tests/api tests/application -q\n956 passed in 45.58s\n```\n\nClass cross-check clean: every new CSS class is emitted by the JS, every emitted class is styled.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **No browser in this environment**, so the badge and the Failover panel are not observed rendering.\n- `route_primary_model` stores the ref the route resolved to first. If a chain falls back twice, the panel attributes the save to the original primary rather than the intermediate that also failed — deliberate, since the question people ask is \"what did my configured model fall back from\", but it does mean a middle link failing is not separately visible.\n- Backfill is impossible: rows before this version never stored the information, so any share over a window spanning the upgrade is computed only over rows that report it.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
